    "available_at": "2026-10-27T19:17:23.365497+00:00",
    "body": "Supply chain teams want AI tools to help them make better decisions, but moving from a demo to daily use takes more than technology . In this Q&A, we asked Brent Hasenkamp , SVP of Sales for North America at o9 Solutions , about what holds companies back, how AI can help with excess inventory , and how planning tools can inform tariff decisions.\nWhat’s Related\nSupply Chain 24/7: What is holding companies back from getting more out of AI?\nBrent Hasenkamp: I think the learnings have been, yes, you can do something quickly, but do you actually have the governance? Do you have the constraints? Do you have the context? And do you really have the skills to make it repeatable, consistent, and something that can actually be governed inside of an organization?\nWe’re seeing that companies that start with a clear statement, then put AI into practice with knowledge agents or tools that can analyze performance after the fact, are able to build on those initial applications.\nRELATED STORIES: Six Things to Consider Before Buying Planning Software | How the Micah Parsons Trade Helped Shape Fanatics’ Inventory Plans\nSC247: How do you see AI changing the work people do?\nHasenkamp : I think whenever we get into a technological revolution, much like we had with the wave of the internet and cloud and what that looks like for business , companies realize they must transform. They have to reimagine roles and understand how they are able to accomplish more than they ever could before.\nAt so many of our client organizations where we’re infusing these AI capabilities, the ultimate goal isn’t massive headcount reduction; it’s to say, how do I spot, detect, and act on things I never would have been able to see earlier. And then ultimately, how am I building more equipped knowledge workers?\nBrent Hasenkamp\n“The ultimate goal isn’t massive headcount reduction; it’s to say, how do I spot, detect, and act on things I never would have been able to see earlier.”\nSC247: Can you give an example of how AI helps with excess inventory?\nHasenkamp : As an example, if the product is valuable enough, and it makes sense, can I move that to a pocket of demand where there is movement? So that’s one decision AI can help with: rebalancing.\nAnother one can be around excess inventory: Do I have anything else coming in? How can I slow it down or stop it altogether because I don’t need it? So that’s a second example.\nThe third one is if I truly can’t move the excess inventory and I don’t have great ideas to create demand, I can send it to my engineering team and can we understand what we can actually use this product for.\nSC247: What common planning problems do you see?\nHasenkamp : I think the organizations that have both the biggest opportunity but also some of the biggest challenges are the ones that have never really had planning in any sort of systematic way before. They’ve largely operated in offline Excel sheets and relied on a lot of tribal knowledge to plan.\nSC247: How are companies using planning tools to respond to tariffs?\nHasenkamp : They’re bringing in revenue, bringing in COGS, and doing all of this work. They were able to use the o9 platform in less than two business days to say, ultimately, here is my exposure, here’s a number of different scenarios because it was also wildly uncertain. And here are the decisions that we would either need to make from a sourcing perspective, or if we don’t change anything, here is the gross margin impact, and here’s the pricing uplift that we would have to have to protect our margin.\nBrent Hasenkamp is SVP of Sales for North America at o9 Solutions .",
